 "context": "<figure id=\"fig-ch07_05_02\"><span data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-id3107799\" data-alt=\"This illustration shows that during glycolysis, glucose is broken down into two pyruvate molecules and, in the process, two NADH are formed from NAD^{+}. During lactic acid fermentation, the two pyruvate molecules are converted into lactate, and NADH is recycled back into NAD^{+}.\">\n<img src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20220509.174553/resources/d9e12aa95d48e46ecf5c0ba608fa8b98b124fee0\" data-media-type=\"image/png\" alt=\"This illustration shows that during glycolysis, glucose is broken down into two pyruvate molecules and, in the process, two NADH are formed from NAD^{+}. During lactic acid fermentation, the two pyruvate molecules are converted into lactate, and NADH is recycled back into NAD^{+}.\" width=\"320\" id=\"6\">\n</span>\n</figure>",
